**Proof of the covariance entries.** The 24 proper cubic rotations are the
signed permutation matrices of determinant one. Under the soldered reading a
rotation `g` sends a recorded neighbour of content `q` to content `gq`, so
the field `h = J Σq` goes to `gh` and a law along the field goes to the law
along `gĥ` with the same odds: any functional of the distribution as a
measure, such as entropy, support size or `1/max`, is unchanged (0e+00 over
the 24 rotations), while the odds of a fixed lab possibility change (by up to
0.400). Under the unsoldered reading the contents do not move and every
functional is invariant. ∎

**Proof of the register entries.** Write `ρ = (1 + r·σ)/2` in the menu basis
along `p`: the diagonal is `(1 ± r·p)/2` and `|ρ₊₋|² = (|r|² − (r·p)²)/4`.
Dephasing keeps `r·p` and removes the rest, so a rate is dephasing-invariant
iff it is a function of `r·p` alone; `1`, `F(p(·|R))`, `F(R)`, `(1 + r·p)/2`
and `2(r·p)²` are, `|r|²` and `1 + |r|² − (r·p)²` are not, and the finite
control over 400 random states agrees (9e-16 against 0.991). ∎

## Theorem 2 — exact statements

**(a) Record-determined rates reduce to the constant clock.** For
D-form-const, D-form-law and D-form-records the rate depends on the site's
surroundings only through the neighbours' records, which change only at
their record events. Between two such events `f ≡ f₀` and the
formation-weighted state is the landed exponential window
`ρ_f = f₀ ∫₀^T e^{−f₀t} ρ(t) dt / (1 − e^{−f₀T})`, so the landed identities
hold with `f₀ = F(p(·|R))` or `F(R)`: the field-menu odds of an isolated
site are `(1 + r₀·ĥ)/2` and the recorded law is the trace rule of `ρ_f`. On
the isolated site there are no neighbour events, and the three functionals
give odds identical to the constant clock at their value to 0e+00 on the
tilted and field menus; in the two-qubit model likewise to 0e+00. ∎

**(b) Closed form of the constant clock on the precessing site.** With
`r(t) = r_∥ + cos(ωt) r_⊥ + sin(ωt) ĥ×r_⊥`, `ω = |h|`, the Laplace
transforms `∫₀^∞ f₀e^{−f₀t} cos ωt dt = f₀²/(f₀²+ω²)` and
`∫₀^∞ f₀e^{−f₀t} sin ωt dt = f₀ω/(f₀²+ω²)` give
`r_f = r_∥ + f₀²/(f₀²+ω²) r_⊥ + f₀ω/(f₀²+ω²) ĥ×r_⊥ + O(e^{−f₀T})`, and the
recorded odds on any menu `p` are `(1 + r_f·p)/2`. The trapezoid quadrature
agrees to 7e-08 over four rates and three menus; on the landed tilted menu
the formula gives 0.8437 for `f₀ = 1` and 0.8154 for `f₀ = |r₀|² = 0.64`, the
landed values. Limits: `f₀ → ∞` gives the initial odds `(1 + r₀·p)/2`
(0.8606 at `f₀ = 10³` against 0.8605); `f₀ → 0` gives the odds of the
field-dephased state, `(1 + (r₀·ĥ)(ĥ·p))/2` (0.7435 at `f₀ = 10⁻³` against
0.7434). On the field menu `r_f·ĥ = r₀·ĥ` for every `f₀`. ∎

So on a non-field menu the odds of every clock depend on the pre-record
transverse component through the dynamics; that dependence is not the
register criterion, which is stated at fixed trajectory in (c).

**(c) The witness pair.** Take `p ⊥ ĥ`, `e₂ = ĥ×p`, and
`r = c ĥ + a p + b e₂`. Precession gives
`r(t) = c ĥ + (a cos ωt − b sin ωt) p + (b cos ωt + a sin ωt) e₂`, so the
menu odds `(1 + a cos ωt − b sin ωt)/2` do not depend on `c`, while the menu
coherence `|ρ₊₋|² = (c² + (b cos ωt + a sin ωt)²)/4` does. Two states with the
same `(a, b)` and `c = 0.565` (the landed `r₀`) against `c = 0.2` have
identical menu odds at every time (1e-16) and coherence magnitudes differing
by at least 0.100. Every registered-only clock gives them identical odds
(1e-16; the constant clock 0.6732), since its window is a functional of the
odds trajectory alone. The purity clock is the constant clock at
`f₀ = a² + b² + c²`, so by (b) its odds `(1 + f₀²/(f₀²+ω²) a −
f₀ω/(f₀²+ω²) b)/2` move with `c`: 0.6126 against 0.5547. The coherence clock
gives 0.7165 against 0.6905. On the field menu the odds are `(1 + c)/2` for
every clock, and the coherence enters only the tick: the purity clock's
conditional mean formation time `1/|r|² − T/(e^{|r|²T} − 1)` reads the
transverse magnitude `a² + b² = 4|ρ₊₋^{(ĥ)}|²`, 1.562 against 2.502 when the
transverse part is halved, while every registered-only clock's mean time is
unchanged (0e+00). ∎

**(d) When D-form-odds is a record-determined clock.** The menu odds obey
`d(r·p)/dt = (h×r)·p`, zero for all `r` iff `p ∥ ĥ`, and for a given `r₀`
also when `r_⊥ = 0`. On the field menu of an isolated site an odds clock is
therefore constant and coincides with D-form-law at the value `F` of the
conserved odds; elsewhere its window `F(d(t)) e^{−∫F(d)}` is not exponential.
In the two-qubit model (site `A` in the field with an unrecorded Heisenberg
partner `B`, the landed parameters) `r_A·ĥ` varies by 0.252 and the odds
clocks give 0.7242 and 0.7535 against the constant clock's 0.7197, while the
law clocks equal the constant clock at their value to 0e+00 (0.7202 and
0.7219 at their own values). ∎

**(e) D-form-time.** A window `F(t) e^{−∫F}` with `F` independent of the
site changes the odds wherever the menu odds vary and not where they are
conserved: the linear hazard with unit mean formation time (1.0000) gives
0.8607 against 0.8437 on the tilted menu and 0.7826 `= (1 + r₀·ĥ)/2` on the
field menu. ∎

